 1 one-third there are three even  numbers two four and six and the  probability of rolling a six given that  it's even is one out of these three  possibilities  so they use the logic that the expected  number of rolls to a 6 given an even  roll would be the reciprocal of this  which is 1 over 1 3 and that would be 3  raws  but this is not the correct answer  because the puzzle is asking for a  slightly different question  it's asking for the expected number of  rolls to six  given that there are even rolls before  the six  this is a slightly different question  and i'm going to show you why the answer  is 1.5  so when you roll a dice there are six  possible outcomes  we need every single roll to be an even  number  and the first roll could be a two for  example  then we roll the dice again and we have  six possibilities and we need this again  to be an even number  so let's say it's a four  when we roll the dice again there's six  possibilities and let's say we get a six  now the sequence will terminate  we have a two four and six and that's it  so we'll be considering sequences of  this form  in other words we're rolling even  numbers which are twos and fours until  we roll a six  the sequence of roles we will be  considering will have the form  e comma e comma so on  until the role of six and in this case e  is equal to two or four  but what's special about six  imagine we rolled until we got a one  if we only had rolls of twos and fours  until we got a one  the sequence of rolls would be e e all  the way until one  and notice the sequence length would  have exactly the same distribution  because rolling until a six is exactly  the same as rolling until a one because  six and one occur with the same  probability  but then there's nothing special to the  role of one  we could also do the same thing until we  roll until a three if we have only twos  or fours until a sequence terminates in  a three this would have exactly the same  distribution as the other sequence