e  two or four rolls until we get a six in  sum roll either the first row the second  roll the third row and so on so i'll  first expand out the numerator into  summation form  and now let's calculate the denominator  we need to know the probability that we  get only twos and fours before we roll a  six in sum roll  this will be the sum from one to  infinity of the probability that we roll  six in roll k  times the probability we only get twos  or fours before roll k which will be the  rolls all the way up to k minus one  now the probability that we roll six in  any particular roll will be one sixth  and then the probability that we roll  only twos and fours up to that roll  will be  the probability two over six which  simplifies to be one third  all the way for each of the k minus one  rolls before we get the six  so now how do we calculate this infinite  series  we can write out several of the terms  and now we have a geometric sequence on  the right  this starts with the term 1 it has a  common ratio of 1 3 so we can calculate  its sum by the formula a divided by the  quantity 1 minus r  substituting in those terms  we get 1 divided by the quantity 1 minus  1 3 which will simplify and simplify  again  and now we can figure out that the  probability we only get 2 or 4 rolls  before we roll a 6 is equal to 1 4.  so this becomes the denominator  and now  we're dividing by 1 4  which will mean  that we're multiplying by 4.  we now have to figure out this summation  but we've already calculated some of  these probabilities  the probability that we roll a 6 on any  particular roll is 1 6  and the probability that we get 2 or 4  up to that roll  will again  be the product of 1 3 to the power of n  minus 1.  so now let's factor out the 1 6 from the  summation  and write out some of the terms of this  summation  so how do we figure out this sum  well it turns out this is kind of like a  geometric series but it's being  multiplied by increasing terms so it's  known as an a